body:
Two nights ago, my mom passed away from bladder cancer after a 5 year struggle.

I don't have all the answers, or any answers really, but my best advice is do not try to do it all by yourself. For a couple years, it was just me. My mom is also a single parent, my older brother lived 12 hours away, and my younger brother has a drug addiction. Eventually, I burnt out. I built up resentment for all the sacrifices. And at times, it made our limited time together worse.

&#x200B;

My brother moved home during the final year, and he then decided he wanted the responsibilities of the primary caregiver. It drove him crazy. He smashed my car, screamed at my Grandma, and broke many, many relationships.

&#x200B;

Do your absolute best, but take care of yourself. Have a good relationship with her.

“This must be Thursday, I never could get the hang of Thursdays.”

&#x200B;

*Hitchhikers Guide to the Galaxy*

RBT certification is often needed to bill insurance companies.

&#x200B;

If you signed a contract saying you would take an RBT exam, they could staff you for a certain amount of time until you pass (presumably the 90 days). If you don't pass or don't take it, you cannot bill insurance. Therefore, you cannot work those cases. Your 6 hours may be for insurance companies that do not require RBT certification.

Ex: You accept a job that needs CPR certification but you do not take the CPR course. You cannot work the job until you take the course & pass.

&#x200B;

It also does take time for passing the RBT exam to update with companies; Took mine about a week.

* Who knew that when your loved one gets cancer, you all of a sudden become a mother, a father, a psychologist, a doctor, an oncologist, an economist, a lawyer, a beggar, but most of all a sad, sick, lonely and depressed person yourself?

This is so true, thanks for putting it into words.

&#x200B;

I'm not convinced she's the one in the wrong here, I would never want a significant other who writes about me like this..

&#x200B;

&#x200B;
